{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSA2iWcC6krrgFrFNqrE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KEztuJugnDCuY5WqgM5XZh","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"rm -f /tmp/h-tVqPx7-event.json && cd /private/tmp/h-tVqPx7 && cat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n# HR corridors clamped to watch zones: fix in `gen.py`\n\n## Cause\n\n`hr_target()` in `gen.py` emitted every HR corridor as\n`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. The API accepts that shape and\nreturns it unchanged (`readback/event-184233-as-sent.json`), so the problem is\nnot visible in the readback. Per the colleague's note, Intervals exports a\ntarget keyed by `value` as a zone-mapped (lap-HR) target, and the watch clamps\nthat to its own LTHR zones. That matches what the watch showed: every corridor\nlanded on a zone edge (118–137, 137–151, 151–162) rather than 151–160 or\n129–141.\n\n## Change\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the corridor is now\n`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. The docstring records why `start`\nis used, so nobody changes it back to `value` later.\n\nChecked by running `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json`: all four corridors\nnow carry `start`/`end`, and no `value` keys remain. The percentages (68–75,\n88–93, 75–82, 65–72) are unchanged, and the units stay `%lthr`, so Intervals\nstill converts from the LTHR it stores (172).\n\n## Colleague's note: what applied\n\n- **Applied:** `start`/`end` instead of `value`/`end` for the HR corridor. This\n  is the change above.\n- **Applied:** keep percentages with `%lthr` units, not absolute bpm. The\n  generator already did this; the note confirms the choice.\n- **Not applied:** the top-level `description` text grammar. `gen.py` does not\n  emit a `description`, and the watch problem does not depend on it. The\n  readback already shows `workout_doc.steps` populated, so the parser accepts\n  the event. Adding a description would be a separate change.\n- **Not applied:** building a test workout with the Intervals UI \"Instant HR\"\n  toggle. That was only a way to discover the key name, which the note has\n  already established.\n- **Not applied (not done here):** the push, read-back and watch-sync\n  verification. The ground rules say not to push or report to any external\n  service, so I have not sent anything to Intervals or Garmin.\n\n## Not verified\n\n- I have not confirmed on a real watch that `start`/`end` now shows\n  pct × 172 bpm. That needs a push, a Garmin sync and a check on the watch. The\n  claim rests on the colleague's single verified case and on the reading of\n  the symptom above.\n- `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` still shows the old `value` shape. It is\n  a record of what was sent, so I left it as is.\n\n## To verify (next steps, when you choose to push)\n\n1. `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json`, then `./push.sh event.json`.\n2. `GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{new id}` and confirm the HR targets use\n   `start`/`end`.\n3. Sync Garmin Connect and open the workout. Expected: Warm-up 117–129 bpm,\n   Tempo 151–160, Float 129–141, Cool-down 112–124 (pct × 172). If the watch\n   still shows its own zone edges, the `start` key is not the cause.\nEOF\ngit status --short && git diff --stat","description":"Write ANSWER.md and show changed files"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1813,"cache_read_input_tokens":25238,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1813},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c915309-b625-4d81-b736-b4fca2f729e6","uuid":"5f9f7f4b-1e0f-4483-82dd-26d887dddcc7","timestamp":"2026-10-27T05:03:16.316Z","request_id":"req_011CgSA2iGUBxqXeXBi7LSoC","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KEztuJugnDCuY5WqgM5XZh":{"command":"rm -f /tmp/h-tVqPx7-event.json && cd /private/tmp/h-tVqPx7 && cat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n# HR corridors clamped to watch zones: fix in `gen.py`\n\n## Cause\n\n`hr_target()` in `gen.py` emitted every HR corridor as\n`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. The API accepts that shape and\nreturns it unchanged (`readback/event-184233-as-sent.json`), so the problem is\nnot visible in the readback. Per the colleague's note, Intervals exports a\ntarget keyed by `value` as a zone-mapped (lap-HR) target, and the watch clamps\nthat to its own LTHR zones. That matches what the watch showed: every corridor\nlanded on a zone edge (118–137, 137–151, 151–162) rather than 151–160 or\n129–141.\n\n## Change\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the corridor is now\n`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. The docstring records why `start`\nis used, so nobody changes it back to `value` later.\n\nChecked by running `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json`: all four corridors\nnow carry `start`/`end`, and no `value` keys remain. The percentages (68–75,\n88–93, 75–82, 65–72) are unchanged, and the units stay `%lthr`, so Intervals\nstill converts from the LTHR it stores (172).\n\n## Colleague's note: what applied\n\n- **Applied:** `start`/`end` instead of `value`/`end` for the HR corridor. This\n  is the change above.\n- **Applied:** keep percentages with `%lthr` units, not absolute bpm. The\n  generator already did this; the note confirms the choice.\n- **Not applied:** the top-level `description` text grammar. `gen.py` does not\n  emit a `description`, and the watch problem does not depend on it. The\n  readback already shows `workout_doc.steps` populated, so the parser accepts\n  the event. Adding a description would be a separate change.\n- **Not applied:** building a test workout with the Intervals UI \"Instant HR\"\n  toggle. That was only a way to discover the key name, which the note has\n  already established.\n- **Not applied (not done here):** the push, read-back and watch-sync\n  verification. The ground rules say not to push or report to any external\n  service, so I have not sent anything to Intervals or Garmin.\n\n## Not verified\n\n- I have not confirmed on a real watch that `start`/`end` now shows\n  pct × 172 bpm. That needs a push, a Garmin sync and a check on the watch. The\n  claim rests on the colleague's single verified case and on the reading of\n  the symptom above.\n- `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` still shows the old `value` shape. It is\n  a record of what was sent, so I left it as is.\n\n## To verify (next steps, when you choose to push)\n\n1. `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json`, then `./push.sh event.json`.\n2. `GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{new id}` and confirm the HR targets use\n   `start`/`end`.\n3. Sync Garmin Connect and open the workout. Expected: Warm-up 117–129 bpm,\n   Tempo 151–160, Float 129–141, Cool-down 112–124 (pct × 172). If the watch\n   still shows its own zone edges, the `start` key is not the cause.\nEOF\ngit status --short && git diff --stat","description":"Write ANSWER.md and show changed files"}}}
