{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSA3HTFKXwgrXBgkn9c4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I changed the generator so HR corridors use `start`/`end` instead of `value`/`end`. I have not confirmed the fix on the watch, because that needs a push and a Garmin sync, and the ground rules say not to send anything to an external service.\n\n**Cause:** `hr_target()` in `gen.py` emitted `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. The API accepts that and returns it unchanged, so the readback looked fine. According to the colleague's note, Intervals exports a `value`-keyed target as a zone-mapped target, which the watch clamps to its own zones. That fits the symptom: every corridor landed on a watch zone edge.\n\n**Change:** `gen.py` now emits `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`, with a docstring explaining why `start` is used. I ran the generator on `plans/tempo-run.json`. All four corridors now use `start`/`end`, no `value` keys remain, and the percentages are unchanged.\n\n**From the colleague's note:**\n- **Applied:** the `start`/`end` key, and keeping percentages with `%lthr` units.\n- **Not applied:** the `description` text grammar, since `gen.py` doesn't emit one and the watch problem doesn't depend on it.\n- **Not applied:** the Intervals UI \"Instant HR\" trick, which was only a way to find the key name.\n- **Not done:** the push, readback, and watch check, because of the ground rules.\n\nThe details, including the expected watch values (117–129, 151–160, 129–141, 112–124 bpm) and the steps to verify, are in `ANSWER.md`. `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` still shows the old `value` shape. It's a record of what was sent, so I left it alone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1599,"cache_read_input_tokens":27051,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemera